WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.480
<v Pledgivo>Post-install setup walkthrough

2
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:34.430
<v Start here>The Get Started checklist — Seven steps, each one graded against your own org

3
00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:47.530
<v Start here>Steps 3 to 5 — Read the evidence line under each step - it quotes your org back to you

4
00:00:47.530 --> 00:01:02.310
<v Start here>Steps 6, 7 and the optional one — Background jobs and branding are already done by the install

5
00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:16.460
<v 1. Permission sets>Four sets ship with the package — Look, don't change - none of them is assigned to anyone yet

6
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:27.930
<v 1. Permission sets>Start from the user, not the set — Open the person, then Permission Set Assignments > Edit Assignments

7
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:44.430
<v 2. The donation site>The Site & Domain checklist — Seven steps, and it says plainly which ones it cannot verify

8
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:50.430
<v 2. The donation site>No site yet — Click New

9
00:01:50.430 --> 00:02:05.830
<v 2. The donation site>The template gallery — Choose Build Your Own (LWR) - the package does not target Aura

10
00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:21.450
<v 2. The donation site>Two fields, and that is all — A name and a short, permanent URL path

11
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:36.980
<v 2. The donation site>Experience Workspaces — Builder designs pages; Administration is the only place with Activate

12
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:48.730
<v 2. The donation site>Experience Builder — Dismiss the Salesforce release dialog, then open the gear icon

13
00:02:48.730 --> 00:03:00.150
<v 2. The donation site>Settings > General — Template, public access and published status all live on this screen

14
00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:19.400
<v 2. The donation site>Let the public in — Tick guest users can see and interact with the site without logging in

15
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:38.400
<v 3. The guest user>The route that works — Workspaces > Administration > Pages > Go to Force.com > Public Access Settings

16
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:53.550
<v 3. The guest user>Assign Fundraising Guest Donor — The packaged set - every object a public donation page reads

17
00:03:53.550 --> 00:04:17.970
<v 3. The guest user>The list view error is not your fault — Only Recently Viewed is offered - open the guest user first

18
00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:27.750
<v 3. The guest user>Pick the guest user — Read the name before you tick it

19
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:40.150
<v 3. The guest user>No expiration date — An expiring grant is a donation page that stops working on an unrecorded date

20
00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:55.870
<v 3. The guest user>Guest User License, Success — The licence column is the proof the set was assignable at all

21
00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:13.420
<v 3. The guest user>Re-check the panel — Can't verify is the honest answer, not a warning

22
00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:29.890
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>Eleven steps, four stages — Keep this panel open in one tab and work in Setup in another

23
00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:42.060
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>Do this next — One card, one move, read from the same org state as the steps below

24
00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:56.760
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>You build two pairs, not one — Walk the same six steps twice - once per key

25
00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:14.780
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>Why nothing is graded yet — These checks read the credential a payment account names

26
00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:30.000
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>Grant it, then connect and test — Both proof buttons stay disabled, with the reason printed beside them

27
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:43.170
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>New external credential — Authentication Protocol: Custom - Stripe uses a bearer token, not OAuth

28
00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:59.890
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>Save, and the related lists appear — Principals and Custom Headers only exist after the first save

29
00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:23.490
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>The principal holds the secret — Parameter SecretKey, value 'Bearer' then your restricted key

30
00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:45.910
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>The Authorization header — Its value is the credential merge field - copy the name off the record

31
00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:58.690
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>The admin credential — Same shape, different key, its own principal

32
00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:15.290
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>New named credential — URL https://api.stripe.com, no trailing path

33
00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:38.760
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>The two checkboxes that decide it — Allow Formulas ON. Generate Authorization Header OFF.

34
00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:47.610
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>The admin named credential — Same URL, same two corrections, pointed at the admin external credential

35
00:08:47.610 --> 00:09:09.560
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>Allowed Namespaces for Callouts — Without the package namespace here, packaged code cannot see the credential

36
00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:22.030
<v 4. The Stripe credentials>Both credentials exist — This is the state that unlocks the Add account button

37
00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:47.930
<v 5. Grant the credential>The page nobody finds — It is under Apps, not Object Settings

38
00:09:47.930 --> 00:10:01.600
<v 5. Grant the credential>Granted — Keep public and admin in separate sets

39
00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:28.650
<v 5. Grant the credential>The guest user's three sets — Guest Donor, Campaign read, and the public credential. No more.

40
00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:42.150
<v 6. The payment account>Add account — A credential is plumbing; this record is what a campaign charges through

41
00:10:42.150 --> 00:11:05.870
<v 6. The payment account>Public in the public slot — Swapping them hands your guest user a refund-capable key

42
00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:18.840
<v 6. The payment account>Ready to take payments — 9 done, 0 to do, 2 to prove

43
00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:30.510
<v 6. The payment account>Stage 2, now graded — The credentials did not change - the account gave the checks something to read

44
00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:44.560
<v 6. The payment account>The two live proofs — Every value in the reference table is a placeholder, never a real key

45
00:11:44.560 --> 00:12:03.080
<v 6. The payment account>Callout options, confirmed — Read back from your credential, not assumed

46
00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:18.730
<v 6. The payment account>Prove the public key is restricted — The only check that can catch a full secret key in the public slot

47
00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:37.280
<v 6. The payment account>Connection test passed — A real callout to Stripe, under the credential you built

48
00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:48.000
<v 6. The payment account>The accounts table — Most orgs need exactly one row here

49
00:12:48.000 --> 00:13:04.420
<v 7. Publish and activate>Publish from Builder — A change that is saved but not published is not on the live site

50
00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:21.370
<v 7. Publish and activate>Note the URL — This dialog prints the real public path - keep it

51
00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:43.720
<v 7. Publish and activate>Published is not the same as Live — Activate lives in Workspaces > Administration > Settings

52
00:13:43.720 --> 00:14:08.640
<v 8. Finishing touches>Third-party content — Optional, and it never counts against your seven steps

53
00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:27.690
<v 8. Finishing touches>Embed it on your own website — Allowed embed origins is an allowlist, not a formality

54
00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:48.310
<v Wrap up>Your org is ready to take donations.

55
00:14:48.310 --> 00:15:15.630
<v About Pledgivo>Fundraising and donation management, native to Salesforce. Docs: cloudalgo.com/pledgivo-docs

